# OpenDiving API configuration.
#
#   cp src/.env.example src/.env
#
# Then generate a SECRET_KEY and change POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Everything uncommented below is
# enough to bring the stack up with `docker compose up`; the commented-out settings show
# their built-in defaults and only need touching if you want something else.
# `src/app/core/config.py` is the full, authoritative list.
#
# Two of the settings here refuse to start the app rather than run on a value copied from
# a template - SECRET_KEY below, and ADMIN_PASSWORD if you enable the admin panel in
# production. Anything commented out is commented out on purpose; uncommenting a block is
# a decision, not a formality.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is the exception, and worth knowing about: docker compose feeds this
# same file to the `db` container, so the POSTGRES_* values below are both the app's
# connection settings and the credentials Postgres initializes itself with. `change-me`
# therefore is a live password the moment you run `docker compose up` - nothing rejects
# it, because rejecting it would stop the local stack coming up at all. It is survivable
# locally only because compose binds Postgres to 127.0.0.1; change it before this
# database is reachable from anywhere else.

# -------- Application (OpenAPI metadata) --------
# Published in the `info` block of /api/v1/openapi.json on every instance, production included.
# APP_NAME="OpenDiving"
APP_DESCRIPTION="Backend API for OpenDiving."
# LICENSE="AGPL-3.0"
# Who maintains this API. Metadata only - this is NOT where the support form delivers
# to; that's CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL near the bottom.
# CONTACT_NAME="Your Name"
# CONTACT_EMAIL="you@example.com"

# -------- Database --------
POSTGRES_USER="postgres"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD="change-me"
# "db" is the compose service name. Use "localhost" when running the API outside docker.
POSTGRES_SERVER="db"
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_DB="opendive"
# The API runs `alembic upgrade head` on startup, so an upgrade is `docker compose pull &&
# docker compose up -d` and nothing else. Turn it off only if you would rather run the
# migrations yourself (`docker compose run --rm api alembic upgrade head`) - the app then
# boots against whatever schema it finds, and a version behind will fail on the first query
# that needs the new column.
# MIGRATE_ON_START=true

# -------- Security --------
# Signs access/refresh/onboarding tokens, so anyone holding this value can mint one for
# any account. Generate your own - the placeholder below is published in this repository
# and the app REFUSES TO START on it, in every environment:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it later signs every existing session out. API keys are not signed with it, so
# they survive a rotation and are revoked one by one, by their owners in Settings.
SECRET_KEY="change-me-openssl-rand-hex-32"
# ALGORITHM="HS256"
# ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# How long a session may sit unused before it ends. Every refresh restarts the clock, so a
# browser in daily use never expires. The extended window is for a session that asks for it
# at sign-in - the iOS app does, a browser never does - and is also how long a create sent
# with an Idempotency-Key is remembered, so a retry of it makes nothing twice.
# REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7
# EXTENDED_REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=90
# A refresh retried within this many seconds of the first - its reply lost on a bad
# connection - gets the same tokens back instead of signing the session out as a replay.
# Raising it widens the window in which a stolen cookie is honoured instead of ending the
# session. It cannot be switched off: below 5 the app refuses to start, because the same line
# keeps two tabs refreshing at once from signing the diver out.
# REFRESH_TOKEN_REUSE_GRACE_SECONDS=60
# ONBOARDING_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# How long a parsed dive-computer file stays attachable to a dive. Long on purpose - a
# diver may import a file, then spend an evening filling in sites and gear before saving.
# DIVE_FILE_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=1440
#
# The refresh cookie is Secure, so it is only sent back over HTTPS. Set this to false ONLY
# for an instance served over plain HTTP - a LAN address with no certificate - where the
# browser otherwise drops the cookie silently and every reload signs the user out.
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# -------- Logging --------
# DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL, applied to the API and the worker alike. An
# unrecognised value fails at startup rather than at the first log line.
# LOG_LEVEL="INFO"

# -------- Redis (cache + arq queue) --------
REDIS_CACHE_HOST="redis"
REDIS_CACHE_PORT=6379
REDIS_QUEUE_HOST="redis"
REDIS_QUEUE_PORT=6379
# One password for both pools. The bundled `redis` container needs none; a managed Redis
# or one running with `requirepass` does.
# REDIS_PASSWORD=""
# CLIENT_CACHE_MAX_AGE=60

# -------- Admin panel login --------
# The login for the CRUDAdmin interface at /admin (which is off by default - see the
# bottom of this file).
# ADMIN_PASSWORD has no default. Leave it unset and no admin account is created at all;
# set it and you get one with full create/update/delete over every model. On any
# ENVIRONMENT but "local" with CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED=true, startup fails unless this is a
# real password - not the value below, nor a stock stand-in.
# ADMIN_USERNAME="admin"
# ADMIN_PASSWORD="a-real-password-not-this-one"

# -------- Environment --------
# local | staging | production. Controls the interactive docs: open at /docs on "local",
# superuser-only on "staging", entirely absent on "production". Also makes SMTP_HOST
# mandatory on anything but "local": sign-in is passwordless, so an instance with no relay
# cannot let anybody in, including its first user, and it fails at startup rather than at
# that discovery. "local" is the only value that falls back to writing the sign-in link to
# the logs, because that link is a live credential and those logs are read by more than
# one person anywhere else.
ENVIRONMENT="local"

# -------- Reverse proxy --------
# Addresses or CIDR blocks of proxies in front of this app whose X-Forwarded-For header
# should be believed. Every per-IP rate limit depends on it.
#
# Leave unset when the app is reached directly (including plain `docker compose up`,
# where the API is published straight to the host) - the socket peer is then the real
# caller and the header must NOT be trusted, or anyone can forge an address per request
# and skip the limits.
#
# Set it when you put nginx/an ALB/Cloudflare in front, otherwise the opposite failure
# applies: every caller looks like the proxy, all the per-IP buckets merge into one, and
# a single bot exhausting the magic-link limit locks sign-in out for everybody. For a
# proxy on a default Docker bridge network that is the container subnet:
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS="172.16.0.0/12"

# -------- Frontend --------
# Used to build the links in outgoing email ({FRONTEND_URL}/auth/verify?token=...) and,
# just as importantly, as the single allowed CORS origin - a browser that can load the
# web app but gets CORS errors from the API is almost always this being wrong. Left at
# the default on a real deployment, every emailed sign-in link points at localhost.
#
# It is also the passkey domain - see the Passkeys section below before changing its
# hostname, which silently orphans every passkey already registered.
FRONTEND_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# -------- Registration (who may create an account) --------
# "invite" (the default) or "open". On "invite" only an address somebody already invited,
# or somebody who follows a member's invite link, can create an account: a stranger who
# lands on the home page gets a "request an invite" form instead of a sign-in form, and
# their address joins a queue the operator works through. On "open" anybody who proves
# they own an address gets an account, which is what this app did before the setting
# existed - uncomment the line below for that.
#
# **The first account on an empty instance is admitted whichever value this has**, and is
# created with operator rights. That is what "the first account to sign in is yours" means
# and it is why closed-by-default is not a first-run trap: sign in once, and you can invite
# everybody else from Settings. It fires on emptiness rather than once, so an instance whose
# last account is deleted and purged hands the next signer-in the same deal.
#
# Leave it closed if this instance is reachable from the internet and you only mean to share
# it with a handful of friends.
# REGISTRATION_MODE="open"

# Whether the OpenDiving project itself operates this instance. A self-hosted install never
# sets this: left alone, the "request an invite" form on the home page carries wording that
# is true of any instance, and an invitation email tells the invitee they were invited to
# your OpenDiving log book. The one instance the project runs sets it, and that form becomes
# a waitlist and says so ("join the waitlist", "we'll notify you when your spot is ready"),
# while the invitation says they were invited to OpenDiving itself. It changes wording and
# nothing else. The form only exists where REGISTRATION_MODE is "invite"; the invitation
# email is sent in both modes, so that half applies either way.
# PROJECT_OPERATED=true

# Whether this instance is the one where OpenDiving Commons is edited. A self-hosted install
# never sets this: left alone, its Commons sites are a read-only copy of what the image ships,
# refreshed by upgrading. The one instance the project runs sets it; there the database holds
# the Commons and GET /commons/dive-sites publishes it.
# COMMONS_PRIMARY=true
# What one address may ask of that route, the whole Commons per request.
# COMMONS_EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# COMMONS_EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=20
# How many Commons proposals one account may send in a day.
# COMMONS_PROPOSAL_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=86400
# COMMONS_PROPOSAL_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=30
# What one account may ask of the dive-site search, which a search box asks once per keystroke.
# DIVE_SITE_SEARCH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=300
# DIVE_SITE_SEARCH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=300
# How many seconds that search waits on the geocoder for its places before it answers without
# them. A typed search also spends the GEOCODER_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER budget below, and a spent
# budget costs the places, never the search.
# DIVE_SITE_SEARCH_GEOCODER_BUDGET_SECONDS=2

# The OpenDiving project's own setting, like PROJECT_OPERATED above: a self-hosted install
# never sets this. Comma-separated slug=Label pairs; unset or empty is the default.
# JOIN_CHANNELS=

# How many invitations one member may make, and over how long. A rate rather than a
# lifetime allotment: at the defaults somebody who invites five friends today can invite
# five more tomorrow. Counted from the invitations table, revoked ones included, so the
# bound is on invitations made - typed by address, or somebody signing up through the
# member's invite link. Superusers are exempt.
# INVITATIONS_PER_USER=5
# INVITATIONS_WINDOW_DAYS=1

# A separate backstop on how often one member may *attempt* an invitation, over the
# magic-link window. Not the same thing as the quota above and deliberately looser: sending
# an invitation to an address that already has an account is refused without creating
# anything, so the quota never charges for it, and without this a signed-in caller could
# probe the endpoint to learn which addresses are registered. Superusers are exempt from the
# quota but not from this.
# INVITATION_ATTEMPT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=20

# Fixed-window rate limits on POST /api/v1/invite-requests, keyed by submitted email and by
# client IP. That endpoint is unauthenticated and writes a row on an anonymous caller's say
# so, which is what these are for. Only consulted when REGISTRATION_MODE is "invite".
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=10

# -------- Email (SMTP) --------
# Leave SMTP_HOST unset for local development: nothing is sent, and the magic-link URL
# is written to the API logs instead, which is the intended local sign-in flow.
# ENVIRONMENT="local" is the only value that allows that - on anything else, staging
# included, startup fails without a relay - see above.
#
# Point SMTP_* at any relay you trust - your provider, your host's relay, or your own
# existing mail server. Don't stand up an MTA for this unless you already know why:
# sending mail is easy, getting it *accepted* (PTR, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, IP reputation, a
# port 25 your host probably blocks) is the hard part, and it's why every serious
# self-hosted app asks for relay credentials instead of bundling Postfix.
#
# SMTP_HOST="smtp.example.com"
# SMTP_PORT=587
# starttls (587, the default) | tls (465) | none (a relay on localhost or the compose
# network - nothing else). Certificates are verified in both TLS modes.
# SMTP_TLS_MODE="starttls"
# Both optional and independent: an anonymous relay needs neither. The login is only
# attempted when SMTP_USERNAME is set.
# SMTP_USERNAME="..."
# SMTP_PASSWORD="..."
#
# Resend users: SMTP_HOST="smtp.resend.com", SMTP_USERNAME="resend", and SMTP_PASSWORD
# is the API key. Mail still shows up in the Resend dashboard.
#
# Reading mail in a local inbox instead: `docker compose --profile mail up` and set
#   SMTP_HOST="mailpit"
#   SMTP_PORT=1025
#   SMTP_TLS_MODE="none"
# then open http://localhost:8025. To go back to logged links, comment SMTP_HOST out
# again - left set with no Mailpit running, sends fail against a dead host and no link
# is logged either. Any edit to this file needs `docker compose up -d --force-recreate
# api` to take effect - a plain `restart` reuses the environment the container was
# created with.

# Required as soon as SMTP_HOST is set - startup fails without it, deliberately. There
# is no sane default: it has to be an address on a domain your relay is allowed to send
# for, and a wrong one fails at delivery time, hours later, in someone's spam folder.
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS="noreply@yourdomain.example"

# -------- Support form (POST /api/v1/support) --------
# Where the web app's support form delivers to. No default and none possible: it used to
# reach the upstream project's own inbox, which meant a self-hosted instance quietly
# forwarded its users' support mail to strangers who can neither see that server nor help
# them. Unset, the endpoint answers 503 and the form is simply off. Delivery also needs
# SMTP_HOST above.
# CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL="you@example.com"
# Fixed-window rate limits on that endpoint, keyed by submitted email and by client IP.
# It's unauthenticated and sends mail, so these are what keep it from being used as a relay.
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=10

# -------- Geocoding (GET /api/v1/geocode/*) --------
# Turns a map pin into "Dahab, South Sinai, Egypt", and "ko tao" typed into a search into a
# position.
# Proxied server-side so no key ever reaches the browser and the web app's CSP needs no
# extra host. Works out of the box with no account.
#
# Two providers, one per question: GEOCODER_URL names the spot behind a pin, and
# GEOCODER_SEARCH_URL answers a place typed into a search.
#
# PRIVACY: on a cache miss, the pinned coordinate is sent to the GEOCODER_URL host and the
# typed search string to the GEOCODER_SEARCH_URL host - third parties you have no agreement
# with. Nothing identifying the diver goes with either (no account, dive, token; the source
# IP is your server's), and caching means it happens once per place rather than once per
# page view. But if you publish a privacy policy, this belongs in it.
#
# Must be a Nominatim-compatible host - that's the wire format the client speaks. Point it
# at your own Nominatim to keep pins in-house - typed searches still go to GEOCODER_SEARCH_URL
# below, public Photon unless it names a host of yours - or set it to "" to switch geocoding
# off entirely, place search included; the endpoints then answer "no result" instead of
# failing, which is the same thing they do when a provider is unreachable.
# GEOCODER_URL="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org"
# Only for a hosted mirror that wants one (sent to GEOCODER_URL as the `key` query
# parameter, never to the search host). The public Nominatim instance is keyless.
# GEOCODER_API_KEY=""
#
# Must be a Photon-compatible host (https://github.com/komoot/photon). Search does not use
# Nominatim: it matches whole words only, and its usage policy forbids search-as-you-type,
# which is what Photon is built for. The default is the public instance komoot runs, a demo
# server with no availability guarantee - when it fails, search answers nothing and a diver
# types the place in. Point it at your own Photon, or set it to "" to switch place search
# off while pins are still named.
# GEOCODER_SEARCH_URL="https://photon.komoot.io"
#
# Language the providers answer in, and it is asked for explicitly because the alternative
# is not "no preference" - it's the local script. Unasked, the Blue Hole reverse-geocodes
# to "دهب, جنوب سيناء, مصر", which is then what lands in the dive site's location. One value
# for the whole instance, not per caller: it's part of the cache key. Photon's public
# instance speaks only de, en and fr, so search asks in English for anything else.
# GEOCODER_LANGUAGE="en"
#
# Nominatim's usage policy REQUIRES a User-Agent that identifies the application and
# blocks generic ones, and Photon's maintainers give a generic one no guarantees. Sent to
# both. If you run a public instance of your own, put your own project and contact address
# here - it is where the providers' operators will get in touch before they block you.
# GEOCODER_USER_AGENT="OpenDiving (+https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving-api)"
#
# The rest of Nominatim's policy is caching (every answer is held in Redis - a month for a
# hit, an hour for a miss) and a cap of one request per second, which the two limits below
# enforce. They behave differently on purpose. The per-user one 429s the account that spent
# it. The provider one is counted across everybody, once for each provider, so hitting it
# skips the call and answers "no suggestion" rather than rejecting whoever happened to
# arrive second. Running your own Nominatim? Raise the provider limit only if
# GEOCODER_SEARCH_URL also names a host you run - the one setting paces both providers.
# GEOCODER_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# GEOCODER_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=600
# GEOCODER_PROVIDER_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=1
# GEOCODER_PROVIDER_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=1
#
# "No suggestion" there means exactly that, and it is a contract rather than an implementation
# detail: /geocode/reverse answers 200 with a null body whenever this instance could not ask -
# over the provider cap, provider unreachable, or geocoding switched off - and 204 only when the
# provider was asked and the position has no name. Clients clear a typed-in location on the 204
# and leave it alone on the null, so setting these too low costs suggestions, never data.
#
# Every result carries the attribution for its data - the provider's own where it sends one,
# OpenStreetMap's where it does not, which Photon never does - and the clients are expected to
# show it. For OpenStreetMap data that is a licence condition.
#
# One exception to all of the above: a pin in genuinely open water is named ("Red Sea")
# from public-domain polygons carried in this repo, with no provider involved - no request
# leaves, no key is needed and no rate limit applies. Nominatim has no row for such a
# point, which is why it is done locally. It is a fallback only: coastal water falls inside
# an admin boundary and still gets the better answer ("Bali, Indonesia", not "Bali Sea").
# Setting GEOCODER_URL="" switches this off along with everything else - disabled means
# disabled, not "the offshore half still runs".

# -------- Seafloor (GET /api/v1/seafloor) --------
# The depth grid the seafloor explorer draws around a point, fetched server-side from public
# bathymetry services: NOAA's coastal elevation models, EMODnet, KHOA, GMRT and ETOPO 2022, with
# OpenFreeMap's coastline and AWS Terrain Tiles' land heights for KHOA's soundings. All of them
# are keyless but KHOA, so the feature works with nothing set here.
#
# PRIVACY: on a cache miss, the point (rounded to about 10 m) is sent to those services. Nothing
# identifying the diver goes with it, and an answer is cached for a week for everyone.
#
# The data.go.kr service key for the Korea Hydrographic and Oceanographic Agency's water depth
# API, the finest source in Korean waters (https://www.data.go.kr, "water depth"). Unset, KHOA is
# reported as not configured and Korean waters are answered by the global sources. Paste it as
# issued, encoded or decoded. Its quota is 10,000 requests a day; a view spends one or two.
# KHOA_API_KEY=""
#
# What one account may ask of it, cache hits included; past it the endpoint answers 429.
# SEAFLOOR_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=120
# SEAFLOOR_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# -------- Surface conditions (the worker's fetch_dive_conditions) --------
# Fills a dive's air temperature, weather and waves where the diver left them empty, from the
# weather, sea state and tide at the dive's place and hour, and shows the rest of what came
# back on the dive page. A filled field is marked as fetched until the diver types another
# value. Off by default: empty asks nobody. "open-meteo" is the one provider.
#
# PRIVACY: once this is set, the worker sends Open-Meteo, a third party, a dive's position
# rounded to two decimals (about a kilometre) and its day, once per place and day on a cache
# miss - and so does the dive form's preview, for the time and place being typed, before the
# dive is saved. Nothing identifying the diver goes with it (no account, dive, name; the source
# IP is your server's). If you publish a privacy policy, this belongs in it.
#
# Open-Meteo's free API is for non-commercial use only - see https://open-meteo.com/en/terms -
# and its data is CC BY 4.0, which the dive page credits.
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_PROVIDER=""
#
# A commercial operator subscribes and sets a key, which moves every call to Open-Meteo's
# customer hosts. The plan must include the Historical Weather API (Professional or above): a
# key without it is refused on the archive and nothing is filled.
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_OPEN_METEO_API_KEY=""
#
# What this whole instance may spend on Open-Meteo, counted only on calls that leave. The
# default sits under the free API's hourly, daily and monthly caps at once, with room for
# one instance behind an address. Over it, dives wait for a later run.
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_OPEN_METEO_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=400
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_OPEN_METEO_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
#
# The dive form previews what a save would fill (GET /api/v1/dive-conditions/lookup) and draws
# on the same budget; over it, the preview is a 503 the form ignores. What one account may ask
# of the preview, cache hits included, and the only limit on it that 429s:
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_LOOKUP_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=120
# DIVE_CONDITIONS_LOOKUP_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# -------- Map tiles (GET /api/v1/map-tiles/{theme}/{z}/{x}/{y}, GET /api/v1/map-style/{theme}) --------
# The squares of the map every dive, trip and dive site card and page head is composed from,
# drawn by the map renderer the web image runs as a command of its own, the first time any
# account asks for one, and kept in the file store for the whole instance. Empty, the default,
# draws none, the cards and page heads show water, and the style route answers 404. A
# host:port, as a Render private service's `hostport` gives it, or an http(s) URL; nothing
# outside the stack should reach it.
#
# PRIVACY: with the renderer on, this stack - not the diver's browser - fetches the basemap's
# tiles for those maps, from the provider the web's MAP_* settings name. A tile holds nothing
# of anyone's and is shared by every account, so a stored one answering faster than a drawn
# one tells a member that someone here was shown that region recently.
# MAP_RENDERER_URL=""
#
# Seconds one draw may take, waiting in the renderer's queue included: a margin over a full
# queue at the slowest cold tile. Raise it, never lower it, where the renderer's queue times
# its cold draw time exceeds it, and keep any proxy in front of the API waiting at least as
# long - nginx gives up after 60 seconds by default.
# MAP_RENDERER_TIMEOUT=90
#
# Two limits per account over one window, each answered 429 beyond it. Draws one account may
# start: a first view of ten cards and a page head asks for at most six tiles each, and a tile
# already stored, or one another request is drawing, costs nothing. Tile requests, stored ones
# included: the rate at which an account can probe what this instance has drawn. Style requests
# count here too.
# MAP_RENDERER_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# MAP_RENDERER_DRAW_LIMIT_PER_USER=150
# MAP_RENDERER_REQUEST_LIMIT_PER_USER=1200

# -------- Species catalog (GET /api/v1/species/*) --------
# The "Species spotted" picker searches two public registers live and stores each species
# locally the first time a diver picks it, so the catalog fills itself one sighting at a
# time. No account, no key, no bulk import - WoRMS's full-database download is proprietary
# and its webservice asks not to be harvested, so this app only ever asks about one taxon.
#
# PRIVACY: on a cache miss the typed search string is sent to the two name registers below
# - third parties you have no agreement with. Nothing identifying the diver goes with it (no
# account, dive or token; the source IP is your server's), and answers are cached for a
# month. Wikimedia Commons is asked something different, and never anything a diver typed: a
# file title derived from an AphiaID when a new species is resolved or the photo backfill
# runs, plus a fetch of the image bytes themselves - and, when an admin curates a species'
# photo, the files in its Commons category with their previews. If you publish a privacy
# policy, all three belong in it alongside the geocoder.
#
# WoRMS is the taxonomic authority - scientific names, synonyms, and the accepted-taxon
# mapping every catalog row is keyed on.
# WORMS_API_URL="https://www.marinespecies.org/rest"
# Wikidata (CC0) supplies common names and aliases, matched to WoRMS through property P850.
# It is not optional garnish: WoRMS records exactly one vernacular for the clownfish, in
# Japanese, so without this a diver typing "clownfish" finds nothing.
# WIKIDATA_API_URL="https://www.wikidata.org/w/api.php"
# Wikimedia Commons supplies each species' photo: its credit metadata and the URL of one
# scaled copy, which this server fetches and stores in whichever blob store
# FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND selects, so browsers never contact Wikimedia. A file narrower than
# 500 px is refused rather than stored soft, unless an admin pins it. Purely optional - unset
# or unreachable, species simply have no photos and every surface renders the name-only card
# it already has for the majority of the register that has no usable image. Note this
# configures the *metadata* call only: the bytes are only ever fetched from
# thumb.wikimedia.org or upload.wikimedia.org, the two hosts that reply names, both
# hard-coded, because that is an SSRF fence rather than a preference.
# COMMONS_API_URL="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/api.php"
#
# Wikimedia REQUIRES a User-Agent that identifies the application and blocks generic ones;
# WoRMS asks to be told who is calling. One string for both. Running a public instance of
# your own? Put your own project and contact address here.
# SPECIES_USER_AGENT="OpenDiving (+https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving-api)"
#
# Two tiers of limit, behaving differently on purpose - the same split the geocoder uses.
# The per-user one is shared by search, resolve and the dive form's suggestions when they
# reach the search, and 429s the account that spent it. The per-provider ones are counted
# across everybody, so hitting one drops that provider out of the search rather than
# rejecting whoever arrived second; the other still answers.
# SPECIES_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# SPECIES_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=600
# SPECIES_WORMS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# SPECIES_WORMS_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120
# SPECIES_WIKIDATA_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# SPECIES_WIKIDATA_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=300
# SPECIES_COMMONS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# SPECIES_COMMONS_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120
#
# Search degrades and never fails: with the registers unreachable it still answers from the
# local catalog, so a species anyone has ever logged stays findable. Resolving a species
# that is *not* yet local is the one thing that cannot degrade - it 503s, because inventing
# a catalog row without the authoritative record is worse than asking the diver to retry.
# The photo fetch degrades too, and deliberately cannot do anything else: it runs after the
# resolve is decided, on a budget of its own, so no slowness of Commons can change what
# POST /species/resolve answers.

# -------- Google Sign-In (optional) --------
# Both halves of one OAuth 2.0 client from the Google Cloud Console, "Web application"
# type. Set both or neither: with an id and no secret the API refuses to start, naming
# them, because signing in with Google redeems an authorization code at Google's token
# endpoint and that takes the secret.
#
# The id is not a secret - it travels in a URL the visitor can read, and it must match the
# frontend's NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. The secret is one, and stays on the API: it is
# never sent to the browser and never given to the web container.
#
# Register {FRONTEND_URL}/auth/google/callback as an Authorized redirect URI on that same
# client, or every sign-in comes back 401. Nothing of Google's runs in the browser: the
# front and sign-in pages contact Google not at all, and pressing the button navigates out
# to Google. See "Setting up Google sign-in" and "Third-party calls" in
# https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving/blob/main/docs/configuration.md
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your-client-secret"

# -------- Magic-link sign-in --------
# MAGIC_LINK_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=900
# MAGIC_LINK_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# MAGIC_LINK_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=15
# MAGIC_LINK_VERIFY_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=30
# Per-IP over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. Account creation answers whether a
# username is taken, so it is kept low; refresh runs on a timer in every open tab, and an
# office behind one NAT gateway is one IP here.
# AUTH_COMPLETE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=10
# AUTH_REFRESH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=240
# Guesses allowed against the six-digit code the sign-in email prints beside the
# link. Running out spends the code and nothing else - the link in the same email keeps
# working, which is what stops a burnt code from becoming a way to lock someone out.
# SIGN_IN_CODE_ATTEMPTS_MAX=5
# EMAIL_CHANGE_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# EMAIL_CHANGE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=3
# Per user over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS, spent only by a username change:
# answering "not available" says the username exists.
# USERNAME_CHANGE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=5
# Per user over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. Linking a person to an account by
# username says whether that username exists, so it is throttled like choosing one; the
# links a logbook import makes count against it too.
# PERSON_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- Passkeys --------
# Nothing here is required, and there is no on/off switch: the browser's own capability
# detection is the switch, and a server flag would just be a second way for it to be
# wrong. The relying-party id and the expected origin are derived from FRONTEND_URL
# above - which is therefore the passkey domain, and changing its *hostname* orphans
# every passkey already registered (browsers scope credentials by hostname). The magic
# link is the recovery path when that happens.
#
# Browsers only expose WebAuthn in a secure context, so a plain-HTTP instance gets no
# passkeys at all and the UI hides itself. `localhost` counts as secure; a bare IP
# address never does, certificate or not.
#
# How long a started ceremony stays completable. It is consumed on the first verify
# attempt regardless, so this only bounds an abandoned one.
# PASSKEY_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECONDS=600
# PASSKEY_MAX_CREDENTIALS_PER_USER=10
# Per-IP over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. The options ceiling is high because
# one is minted per signed-out page view that supports autofill, and an office behind a
# single NAT gateway is one IP here.
# PASSKEY_OPTIONS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=240
# PASSKEY_VERIFY_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=30
# PASSKEY_REGISTER_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- API keys --------
# A diver makes these in Settings for tools of their own. Expired keys count toward the
# cap until revoked. Creating one sends an email, so creation is limited per account over
# MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS.
# API_KEY_MAX_PER_USER=10
# API_KEY_CREATE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- Full export (GET /api/v1/export/*) --------
# Fixed-window rate limit, keyed per user and **shared across all four export endpoints**
# - the budget bounds total export work, so ten CSV downloads do use up the archive's
# allowance too. Authenticated and owner-only, so this is not an abuse boundary the way
# the support form's is; it's here because one archive request reads every dive-computer
# file, c-card image and picture the caller owns. Generous on purpose: this is a button a diver
# presses once, and a nightly backup of your own account should not hit it.
# EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- Logbook import (POST /api/v1/imports) --------
# Its own budget rather than a share of the export one: an import spools up to half a
# gigabyte, parses a whole logbook and may make outbound WoRMS calls, and a diver
# restoring a backup should not find their next export refused because of it. One import
# is one upload, so this is ten imports an hour; applying them counts apart, on these figures.
# IMPORT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# IMPORT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# How long an uploaded import waits to be applied or discarded, in hours, before it and its
# files are deleted. Blank keeps it until the diver finishes it; a value below 1 refuses
# startup. A change applies to imports uploaded from then on.
# IMPORT_PENDING_LIFETIME_HOURS=24

# How long an applied import's review and log stay in its history, in days. Blank keeps each
# until the diver deletes it; a value below 1 refuses startup. A change applies to imports
# applied from then on.
# IMPORT_HISTORY_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Total wall-clock budget for looking up species the catalog does not hold yet, before an
# import's transaction opens. Sized against the worst case of a single lookup (about a
# minute, on the synonym branch) rather than its typical cost. Species still unresolved
# when it runs out are skipped and reported - never guessed, and never a reason to drop a
# dive.
# IMPORT_SPECIES_BUDGET_SECONDS=120

# -------- File storage (uploaded exports, c-card images, avatars, portraits, species photos, map tiles) --------
# Where uploaded payloads are kept. `local` is the default and is what every compose
# install wants: the bundle mounts a named volume at FILE_STORAGE_DIR, so nothing here
# needs typing and nothing below applies.
#
# `s3` is for a deployment whose disk cannot be shared - a platform where a volume attaches
# to one service at a time, while both the API and the worker need the blobs. Any
# S3-compatible store: Cloudflare R2, MinIO, Garage, Ceph, Backblaze, AWS.
# FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND=local

# Where the `local` backend writes. The default is the path both compose files mount a
# volume at, so leave it alone unless you are bind-mounting a real directory.
# FILE_STORAGE_DIR=/data/files

# The `s3` backend's settings. The first four are required the moment
# FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 - startup refuses and names whichever is missing, rather than
# failing on the first diver's upload.
#
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL is the full origin including the scheme, e.g.
# https://<account-id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com for Cloudflare R2, or
# http://minio:9000 for a MinIO beside this stack.
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# `auto` is what R2 wants and what every other store ignores. A real AWS bucket needs its
# own region here instead.
# S3_REGION=auto

# Optional key prefix, for sharing one bucket between instances. Prepended to every object
# name; the keys the database holds are unchanged either way, so this can be added or
# removed only by moving the objects.
# S3_PREFIX=

# Switching between the two backends is a copy, not a migration - the key a row carries is
# the same string on both. With both configured and the old one still selected:
#     docker compose exec api python -m src.scripts.migrate_blobs --to s3
# then flip FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND and restart. It is resumable, and never deletes the
# source.

# How much one account may store, in MB: its dive-computer files as stored (compressed,
# about a tenth of their size), plus its card scans and pictures. An upload that would go
# past it is refused; nothing stored is ever removed. Blank means no limit; a value below 1
# refuses startup. Map tiles are drawn by this server and count against nobody.
# STORAGE_LIMIT_MB=1024

# -------- Scheduled emails --------
# Hour of day (UTC) the three scheduled emails run: the "your gear is due for service"
# digest, the certification and insurance renewal reminders, and the year in review.
# GEAR_SERVICE_DIGEST_HOUR=7

# -------- Account deletion --------
# How long a deleted account stays restorable before the hourly purge destroys it and
# everything it owns - dives, dive drafts, sites, courses, certifications, contacts, people,
# tags, gear, and the uploaded files behind them. The account is dark from the moment the user presses the
# button either way; this is only how long the way back in stays open.
#
# 0 means the next sweep purges it, with no way back. The confirmation email says so
# rather than telling the user to sign in before a date that has already passed.
#
# The bundled privacy page promises erasure "within 30 days", so raising this past about
# 29 makes that sentence false for your instance - see
# https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving/blob/main/docs/configuration.md
# ACCOUNT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=14
# Per-user over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. DELETE /user sends mail; this bounds
# that, and is not what stops a double submit (the endpoint's database predicate is).
# ACCOUNT_DELETION_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=5

# -------- Admin interface (CRUDAdmin, mounted at /admin) --------
# OFF, and this whole block is commented out on purpose. The panel talks to the models
# directly, bypassing every ownership check in api/v1, so it is a deliberate opt-in rather
# than something a fresh install inherits. Enabling it means uncommenting CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED
# here AND setting a real ADMIN_PASSWORD above; outside local, startup refuses without one.
# Restrict who can reach it while you're at it - comma-separated, both optional:
# CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS="203.0.113.7,203.0.113.8"
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_NETWORKS="10.0.0.0/8"
# CRUD_ADMIN_MOUNT_PATH="/admin"
# CRUD_ADMIN_MAX_SESSIONS=10
# CRUD_ADMIN_SESSION_TIMEOUT=1440
#
# ---- Running the panel with more than one worker ----
# The shipped image runs `gunicorn -w 4`. One setting is required for that, and one
# existing default must not be turned off.
#
# REQUIRED - CRUD_ADMIN_DB_URL: where the panel's own tables live. Unset means a SQLite
# file inside a single container, which every worker races to create and no other
# container can read. Point it at the app's Postgres instead (note the +asyncpg driver):
# CRUD_ADMIN_DB_URL="postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:your-postgres-password@db:5432/opendive"
#
# SESSIONS: admin sessions need a store shared by all workers, or a login handled by one
# worker is not recognised by the next request landing on another and the panel logs you
# straight back out. CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_SESSIONS=true (the default, below) already provides
# this by persisting sessions to the admin_session table, so there is nothing to do -
# just don't set it to false on a multi-worker deployment. CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_ENABLED=true
# is an alternative shared store, useful to keep session lookups off Postgres, but it is
# not required.
#
# Measured with 4 workers: Redis sessions 12/12 requests authenticated, DB-tracked
# sessions 12/12, and both disabled 1/12 - the one request that happened to land on the
# worker holding the session.
#
# Leave all of this unset and the panel works fine on a single worker, which is what
# local development runs via the --reload command in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Session cookies are Secure by default, so they will not survive plain HTTP. Set this
# to false only for local development over http://localhost.
# SESSION_SECURE_COOKIES=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_EVENTS=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_SESSIONS=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_ENABLED=false
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_HOST="localhost"
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_PORT=6379
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_DB=0
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_PASSWORD=""
